WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.646
Render, commit, effect: the three steps behind every change on a React screen.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:13.531
One click calls setCount(count + 1) three times. The screen goes up by one, not three.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.466
To see why, picture a photo booth.

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:20.895
The click gets a snapshot. It never sees what happens next.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.040
A component is a function that returns what the screen should look like.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:31.458
Every time React calls it, that's a render. And every render is one photo.

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:36.956
In this photo, count is frozen at 0. For this render, it's a constant.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:42.874
Even the click handler is stuck in its photo. It keeps seeing 0, however long it lives.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:49.268
A click can't edit the photo. Each setter call just writes a note: make it 1.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:55.001
Three calls, three identical notes. A log straight afterwards still prints 0.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:01:01.175
When the handler returns, React reads every note and develops one new photo. count is 1.

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:07.655
Five setters in one click still mean one render. That's batching: no half-finished photos.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.657
Write the note differently: add one to whatever it is by then.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:18.783
Now the notes chain: 0, 1, 2, 3. The next photo shows 3.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.970
Use this form whenever the next value depends on the previous one.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.368
Developing the photo is the render. It only works out what the screen should look like.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:36.851
Then comes the commit. React pins the photo to the wall, changing only what differs. The wall is the DOM.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.268
Watch the strip below: render, then commit. Every photo goes through both.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.347
The flash goes off, and now effects run. An effect keeps something outside React in sync.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.566
A chat component connects to the "general" room, after the commit.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:58.387
Its dependency array lists the values it reads. Here, the room.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:06.766
Type in a text box, and the room stays the same. React compares, sees no change, and skips the effect.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:13.416
Leave a value out of the array, and the effect keeps using the old one. Fix the reason, never the array.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:19.123
The user switches to "travel". New photo, new commit.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:24.407
Before the new effect runs, React takes down the old one: disconnect from "general".

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:30.246
Only then does it connect to "travel". Stop the old, start the new, always in that order.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:33.918
Close the chat, and the last cleanup runs once more.

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:39.651
In development, StrictMode does this on purpose, to prove your cleanup really undoes your setup.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:45.251
Render, commit, cleanup, effect. Same order, every photo.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:50.597
Practise it with the Render-cycle visualiser in State and effects.
